      memoryVerse:
        '"Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you: For every one that asketh receiveth; and he that seeketh findeth; and to him that knocketh it shall be opened." — Matthew 7:7-8 (KJV)',
      teachingContent:
        '### The Priest\'s Approach: Bringing Our Requests Before God\n\nEach morning and evening, the priest approached the golden altar with purpose. He did not wander aimlessly into the Holy Place; he came with a specific task, carrying burning coals from the altar of sacrifice and the carefully prepared incense. His approach was deliberate, reverent, and expectant. In the same way, supplication — the prayer of asking — is not a vague, directionless hope cast into the void. It is the deliberate, faith-filled bringing of specific needs before the throne of the God who has both the power and the willingness to act. Hebrews 4:16 invites us: "Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need." The word "boldly" in the Greek means with confidence, with freedom of speech, without hesitation. We approach not as beggars but as children invited by a loving Father.\n\n### Jesus\' Extraordinary Invitation\n\nJesus gave His followers the most remarkable invitation to supplicatory prayer ever uttered: "Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you: For every one that asketh receiveth; and he that seeketh findeth; and to him that knocketh it shall be opened" (Matthew 7:7-8). Three verbs — ask, seek, knock — represent an escalating intensity of prayer, and each carries a continuous tense in the original Greek: keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking. Jesus then drives the point home with a parable from everyday life: "What man is there of you, whom if his son ask bread, will he give him a stone? Or if he ask a fish, will he give him a serpent? If ye then, being evil, know how to give good gifts unto your children, how much more shall your Father which is in heaven give good things to them that ask him?" (Matthew 7:9-11). The argument is from the lesser to the greater: if imperfect earthly fathers give good gifts, how much more will our perfect Heavenly Father respond to His children\'s requests.\n\n### The Effectual, Fervent Prayer\n\nJames writes, "The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much" (James 5:16). The phrase "effectual fervent" translates a single Greek word, energeo, from which we get "energy." This is not lukewarm, halfhearted, going-through-the-motions prayer. It is energized, passionate, Spirit-empowered petition that lays hold of the promises of God with unshakeable faith. James immediately illustrates with Elijah, "a man subject to like passions as we are," who prayed earnestly and the rain stopped for three and a half years, then prayed again and the heavens opened. The same God who answered Elijah answers us. The key is not the eloquence of the prayer or the worthiness of the one praying, but the faithfulness of the God who has promised to hear and answer.\n\n### Praying According to God\'s Will\n\nSupplication is not a blank check for selfish desires. We bring our needs to God, but we do so in submission to His wisdom. Paul modeled this balance: "My God shall supply all your need according to his riches in glory by Christ Jesus" (Philippians 4:19). God promises to supply our need, not necessarily our want, and He does so "according to his riches in glory" — meaning His supply is unlimited and His timing is perfect. Ellen White wrote that prayer and faith are closely allied, and they need to be studied together. We pray with boldness because God has invited us to ask. We pray with humility because God\'s wisdom exceeds our own. We pray with expectation because God is faithful to His promises. The priest approached the altar with specific incense on specific coals at specific times — and so we bring specific requests with specific faith at every opportunity God gives us.',
